those whom Almighty God has predestined  to eternal life even before the creation  of the world and while they are still in  the toils of worldly cares and pursuits  he leads with unutterable mercy to the  principles of the perfect life and does  so in many ways that flow from the pure  spring of his clemency so - it seems  appropriate fast to set down in  compendious language the praiseworthy  record of a memorable man whose life has  been exemplary in our times and will  prove beneficial for us as we read and  reread it and will be of equal use and  profit to posterity with the grace of  God I shall tell how by happy adventures  a man called finden a skort frim  Leinster attained perfection of life  despite frequent torments and  temptations  now the sister of this man was taken  captive with other women by those  foreigners called Norse men when they  devastated far and wide in the Scottish  island called Ireland then their father  ordered his son Finn 'dom to ransom her  and bring her back to him burning to  perform his father's orders and guarding  his sister's love in a faithful heart he  gathered companions around him and set  off with an interpreter but early on the  journey he was captured by pagans thrown  into Chains and carried without delay to  their ships which were moored at a beach  nearby all that day and the following  night he suffered in his fetters without  food or drink in the morning the norse  men held the council at which the more  sensible of them inspired in their  humanity by God we believe argued that  men who had come to ransom others ought  not themselves to be held by force and  this is how finden was set free for our  Blessed Lord recognized that his servant  though still a layman would serve him in  the future most devoutly in every way so  - on another occasion when a crowd of  the same enemies had chased him fleeing  into a house none of them were able to  find him hidden behind the door though  they searched for him in every corner  [Music]  now we think should we be silent about  the circumstances of his undertaken a  foreign journey or about the laudable  manner in which he attempted to conclude  it in the province of Leinster serious  dissension arose between two chieftains  the father of the finden who is our  subject was a soldier of one of the  chieftains and he killed the man of the  opposing party the opposing chieftain  overcome with anger advanced on the home  of Finland's father accompanied by a  large force with the intention  of destroying the man and his  possessions with fire and sword they  arrived by night and placed an armed  cordon around the house they set fire to  the roof and murdered Finn Dan's father  as he emerged from the flames finden was  lodged in another house and they started  fires around him in the same way but  could not lay hands on him so valiant  was his defence with the protection of  divine grace he eluded both the flames  and his enemies with scarcely a wound  his brother was in the same house and  they killed him not surprisingly the  event caused fierce emnity and  inexorable feuding between the tribes  before long however through the  intermediacy of some trustworthy men  finden and his people were given a large  sum of money and peace was restored  between the two sides in the very same  year Finland's enemies began to fear  that grief for his father would quicken  again in his heart and that he would  take revenge upon them equally they  wished to do away with him and were  hatching secret plots to this end they  put their plan into action by preparing  a seaside banquet for Finland Finland  was invited but Norseman arrived in the  middle of the feasting by prior  agreement with his enemies and seized  him they bound him tightly and  immediately made off with him  [Music]  as was offered the case his Norse Lord  unwilling to return home yet sold them  on to another who sold one to a third  and third to fourth  this last wanted to see his homeland  again so gathering his companions  together he led finden and some others  into captivity  when they had reached the midpoint of  their sea journey the Norseman chance to  encounter another Norse fleet one of  these people boarded the ship where  Finland was a captive and started asking  about the nature of the island and how  they fared there on board the ship  however was a man whose brother had been  killed by the questioner who was no  sooner recognized than killed as soon as  his companions saw this they got ready  for battle and the two ships engaged in  a long bitter fight while the struggle  was raging finden wishing to support his  master his companions got to his feet  despite his shackles the crews of the  other ships intervened and separated the  combatants finden shipped backed out of  danger Finland's master was mindful of  the loyalty finden had shown despite  being in shackles  wishing to reward such faithfulness he  immediately released finden from his  chains finden showed promise of bringing  blessings to him in the future after  that they reached some islands called  the Orkneys close to the Pictish race  they disembarked to refresh themselves  as they awaited a favorable wind they  wandered here and there around the  islands  when finden saw how free and easiest  captors were he began to explore the  island wandering anxiously how to get  away safely in a lonely area he found a  huge rock and attempted to hide behind  it but the rising tide usually reached  the rocket and finden was at a loss as  to where he should go or what he should  do on one side the sea threatened and on  the other fear of his enemies tormented  him as they ran about the island and  even walked on the rock where he was  hidden calling his name from all sides  he preferred to endure the wild sea  rather than fall into the hands of men  whose savagery outstripped that of  monstrous beasts those starving  he made light of the massive waves and  remained in the same place all that day  and the following night next day his  enemies stayed in another part of the  island and the force of the sea  diminished no longer reaching the  threshold of his refuge though sometimes  driven by the wind it surged right into  his cave so finden arose and fearing the  heathens crept cautiously through the  scrub in search of an escape route he  thought too that the land around him had  human inhabitants  which in the end of the island however  he discovered that on one side lay the  vast sea while the other side was  indented by a shallow bay robbed of his  physical strength and overcome by the  terrible weakness brought on by  imprisonment and hunger finden did not  dare entrust himself to the sea with  redoubled efforts he scoured the island  for three days on end subsisting on  herds and water as he sought a way to  get off and so he remained there until  on the third day he saw sea monsters and  huge dolphins playing and diving close  to the shore overcome by divine mercy he  contemplated these creatures with an  untroubled mind and poured out these  tearful prayers from the bottom of his  heart God creator both of these brute  beasts and of me a human being you gave  them the sea as their elements and to me  you gave the earth on which I set my  feet as is your merciful custom help me  in my present troubles from this moment  I dedicate my service to you in body and  soul and I shall never think again of  the enticements of this world I shall  seek you in the dwellings of the  Apostles  I shall journey into exile I shall never  return to my homeland from this moment I  shall serve you with all my strength and  follow you without a backward glance  fortified by this strong faith he  plunged fully dressed into the sea and  what I shall relate now is miraculous  instantly divine mercy made his clothing  rigid so that its support kept him from  sinking it seemed to him that his  clothes swam and bore him safely to land  through the swollen waves he climbed to  the hilltops looking for houses or smoke  rising from the house roofs for two days  he lived on a scanty diet of vegetation  for the dawn of the third day he saw  people walking about in the distance  though he had no idea who they were his  heart left within him and he went up to  them without hesitation they welcomed  him and took him to the bishop of the  neighboring community this bishop had  studied his letters in Ireland and had a  good knowledge of the language finden  stayed two years receiving many  kindnesses from this humane and generous  man  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

